{"name":"Mountain Laurel","hex":"#f4c8d5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mountain laurel","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any member of the species Kalmia latifolia of flowering evergreen shrubs, in the family Ericaceae, native to the eastern United States, all parts of which are toxic.","Any member of the species Dermatophyllum secundifolium of flowering shrubs, native to Texas and Mexico."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mountain_laurel"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dermatophyllum secundiflorum","blocks":[{"text":"Dermatophyllum secundiflorum is a species of flowering shrub or small tree in the family Fabaceae that is native to the Southwestern United States and Mexico. Its common names include Texas mountain laurel, Texas mescalbean, grape Koolaid tree, frijolito, and frijolillo."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Calia_secundiflora_flowers.jpg/330px-Calia_secundiflora_flowers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dermatophyllum_secundiflorum","wikidata":"Q1664523"},{"title":"Cryptocarya nova-anglica","blocks":[{"text":"Cryptocarya nova-anglica, commonly known as mountain laurel, is a species of flowering plant in the family Lauraceae and is endemic to New South Wales. It is a tree with lance-shaped leaves, creamy green flowers, and elliptic to spherical to pear-shaped black drupes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Cryptocarya_nova-anglica_juvenile.jpg/330px-Cryptocarya_nova-anglica_juvenile.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cryptocarya_nova-anglica","wikidata":"Q31049"},{"title":"Mountain","blocks":[{"text":"A mountain is an elevated portion of the surface of a planet, generally with steep sides that show significant exposed bedrock. Although there are no universally accepted definitions, a mountain is usually considered higher than a hill. By definition, mountains are often thought of as being a landform which is higher than 2,000 feet (610 m). It may either have a limited summit area or be a smaller plateau with high elevation and steep sides. A few mountains are isolated summits, but most occur in mountain ranges."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Everest_North_Face_toward_Base_Camp_Tibet_Luca_Galuzzi_2006.jpg/330px-Everest_North_Face_toward_Base_Camp_Tibet_Luca_Galuzzi_2006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mountain","wikidata":"Q8502"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Texas_mountain_laurel_fruiting_stage.jpg/500px-Texas_mountain_laurel_fruiting_stage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Texas_mountain_laurel_fruiting_stage.jpg/500px-Texas_mountain_laurel_fruiting_stage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A specimen of Dermatophyllum secundiflorum in its fruiting stage.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATexas_mountain_laurel_fruiting_stage.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Cryptocarya_nova-anglica_flower.jpg/500px-Cryptocarya_nova-anglica_flower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Cryptocarya_nova-anglica_flower.jpg/500px-Cryptocarya_nova-anglica_flower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Flower","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACryptocarya_nova-anglica_flower.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Taita_Chimborazo%22_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Volc%C3%A1n_Chimborazo%2C_%22El_Ta